w: why did you a-ban-don it? The rea-son to me tell.
W:
W:O, Father dear, I often hear you speak of Erin's Isle,
W:Her her lofty scenes her valleys green, her mountains rude and wild
W:You said it is a lovely place wherein a prince might dwell,
W:Why have you then forsaken her, the reason to me tell?
W:
W:My son, I loved our native land with energy and pride
W:Until a blight fell on my crops my and sheep and cattle died,
W:The rents and taxes were too high, I could not them redeem,
W:And that's the cruel reason why I left Old Skibbereen.
W:
W:It's well I do remember on a bleak November's day,
W:The landlord and the sheriff came to drive us all away;
W:they set my house on fire with their cursed English spleen
W:And that's another reason why I left Old Skibbereen.
W:
W:Your mother, too, God rest her soul, lay on the snowy ground,
W:She fainted in her anguish seein' the desolation round.
W:She never rose, but passed away from sleep to mortal dream,
W:And found a quiet grave, my boy, in dear old Skibbereen.
W:
W:It's well I do remember the year of forty-eight,
W:When we arose with Erin's boys to fight against our fate;
W:I was hunted through the mountains as a traitor to the Queen,
W:And that's another reason that I left Old Skibbereen.
W:
W:Oh father dear, the day will come when in answer to the call
W:Each Irishman with feeings stern will answer one and all,
W:I'll be the man to lead the van, beneath our flag of green,
W:And loud and high we'll raise the cry," Remember Skibbereen!"